    {
     "slug": "ellensburg-to-beverly-dunes",
     "number": 5,
     "title": "Ellensburg to Beverly Dunes",
     "text": "This one was brutal.  Beautiful still.  An easy enough ride but hot and dry with no pitstops on the way.  There was scant information about the campsite that I was heading to - Beverly Dunes.  No reviews or pictures, just a blip on the map.  But it was the only place in the vicinity where I was going to end up at the end of the day.\n\nSweeping vistas, colourful scrub and grasses.  Harsh and washed out in the mid day sun but soft and magical during the early and late hours.  I was heading ultimately to the Gorge Amphitheater for a music festival.  Photos of the amphitheatre look amazing - a really unique setting.  I had no idea it was in such a desert.  \n\nThe Colombia River was a sight to behold when I found it.  Looking at it didn't cool me down though.  I was hoping to find a convenience store on the other side of Beverly Bridge but couldn't find one.  Nothing on the map.  Just a small immigrant working community.  No easy way to access the river.  Hoping for the best I continued on to the campground.  I had a water filter with me, I just needed a source of water.  \n\nBy the time I got to the campsite I was cooked.  Greeted by a dust tornado I found a bit of shade.  Must've been over 40 degrees.  Set up my tent and crawled into it to escape the heat.  I think I actually started to suffer heat stroke.  Realising I could die there if I didn't find water, and with the bike load lightened, I got back on the road and headed towards the community.  On the way I found a body of water to swim in.  Maybe it was the dregs of the irrigation system, carrying with it all of the fertilisers and runoffs.  It didn't matter, it was that or perish.  Friend of the frogs and the dragonflies, that was the best swim I've ever had.  \n\nI continued to ride after that and found that there was a convenience store right near the base of the bridge.  How about that.  Hydration and beer sorted.  Back to the campsite.  Maybe the most dangerous day that I had throughout the trip, but I was rewarded by the change of light over the dunes as the sun went down.  Incredible...",
