    {
     "slug": "beverly-dunes-to-ancient-lakes",
     "number": 6,
     "title": "Beverly Dunes to Ancient Lakes",
     "text": "Nothing too special to write about this section.  I rode to George.  It's a town near the Gorge Amphitheater.  Guess I was quietly stressed and self distracting about how things were going go when I got to the festival.  Doubting whether riding a bicycle there was a good idea to begin with.  It wasn't really.  But the stupid idea got me out of the house I guess.\n\nNot a terrible ride.  Straight lines and agriculture.  Corn and sprinklers.\n\nThe night before the festival I rode out to what I knew to be a natural feature marked on the map called The Ancient Lakes.  With a name like that, how could you not?  Getting there I followed the RideWithGPS map again and I think I was the first person to do that in a long time.  What do you call bush bashing on bike?\n\nFunny story, I got there and it was the night before Independence Day.  All the best spots were taken by non-white people.  ICE would have had a field day.\n\nNot for me though.  I was legal as an eagle.  Same with this other dude.  Born & bred American.  He rocks up late in the piece, takes the last feasible spot in the area right near me as the sun is setting.  It's getting windy.  He's got a 4b4 and all the gear and is attempting to erect a makeshift castle in defiance of the elements as the night closes in.  Is unable to conquer the elements, gets mad that he is unable to provide shelter for his family, yells at his wife, packs up and leaves!\n\nSpeculating here but the story goes - he goes home, votes for Trump, applauds the roundup of the immigrants that are the backbone of the economy that are working all around him, then continues to live in misery.  \n\nIDK maybe maybe not.  It was a weird place to camp in.  Especially after the ordeal it took to get there and the whiplash ahead of me.  The geography was amazing.  Ancient Lakes.  I'm no Geologist but this whole side of the Cascades appeared to me to be the dregs of ancient glacial erosion and the remaining landscape now sits in a deathly rain shadow, unloved by God's moistness so enjoyed by the other side of the state but a few kilometres away.  Enjoy God's moistness while you can folks coz there's more of this to come.  Desolate beauty but withholding in that sweet hydration that we've all evolved to know and love.",
